  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through Friday)\nIssued at 1233 AM CDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nThe main low and vorticity with the troughing across the Southwest \nCONUS will quickly eject northeastward towards and into the High \nplains through Monday. However, there should be some leftover \nshortwave energy that lingers behind within the southwesterly flow \nthat crosses through Texas Monday through Tuesday night. This will \nbring in some mid-level moisture into play and combines with the \nlower level moisture to squeeze out some low (15-30%) rain chances \nacross the region Monday night through Tuesday. This QPF signal \nremains quite light with little, if any, thunder potential. \n\nThe shortwave exits the region by Wednesday but the region remains \nunderneath a southwesterly flow aloft. several of the medium range \nguidance does continue to spit out some spotty qpf Wednesday \nafternoon across mostly the Coastal Plains with the lingering \nmoisture, perhaps with a seabreeze or wind surge. Rain chances are \nthen forecast to climb late week. This would be due to multiple \nperturbations arriving within the Southwesterly flow aloft. The \nlatest medium range guidance has become a bit more shy in regards \nwith the main vorticity and moisture from Hurricane Vance being \ninvolved, but still does show some pieces of energy and moisture \npossibly stripping off the tropical cyclone and perhaps becoming \nabsorbed into the arriving perturbations within the southwesterly \nflow aloft. The QPF signal is not overly impressive across our area \nbut is certainty better than with the rain chances earlier in the \nweek.\n\nTemperatures remain warm and steady through next week with daytime \nhighs mostly ranging from the mid 70s to the mid 80s. Pending how \nthick cloud cover and how much rain coverage becomes, a day or two \nmay trend a little lower. Overnights remain mild and a bit humid \nwith lows ranging mostly from the mid 50s to the mid 60s.",
